1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,720
Hi everyone.

2
00:00:02,120 --> 00:00:13,700
Today I want to introduce you to a wonderful chip that has become widespread, on which you can assemble galvanically isolated converters with flyback topology, that is, a flyback converter.

3
00:00:14,860 --> 00:00:26,500
I got acquainted with this chip by chance during a trip to an internet acquaintance, and there we encountered the issue that this converter was under voltage at the output.

4
00:00:29,920 --> 00:00:36,540
Working it out in the field was unrealistic, there was no necessary equipment, and no skills for working with this chip.

5
00:00:37,280 --> 00:00:41,840
And after arriving home I decided to master it, since this chip has quite a lot of potential.

6
00:00:43,120 --> 00:00:44,860
What can be noted about this chip?

7
00:00:47,220 --> 00:00:48,820
It's made in different packages.

8
00:00:51,340 --> 00:00:53,320
I prefer to work in DIP packages.

9
00:00:55,020 --> 00:00:56,700
It's small here, 8 pin.

10
00:00:57,740 --> 00:01:01,520
There's also a surface-mount SMD version in the same kind of package.

11
00:01:02,140 --> 00:01:08,680
The main circuit elements will be the controller chip itself, the switch, the converter core, and a high-speed Schottky diode.

12
00:01:15,900 --> 00:01:20,120
I want to note that in this converter this part isn't a transformer, but a kind of choke.

13
00:01:23,990 --> 00:01:28,630
On switching of the choke's primary winding with the switch, a sharp kick happens.

14
00:01:32,500 --> 00:01:40,080
The choke stores energy in the core as a magnetic field, and then during the pause dumps it into the load through the secondary winding.

15
00:01:43,520 --> 00:01:45,160
The chip has two feedbacks.

16
00:01:48,390 --> 00:01:53,490
First, voltage feedback, implemented through a PC817 optocoupler.

17
00:01:54,330 --> 00:01:56,030
And second, current feedback.

18
00:01:58,790 --> 00:02:02,190
It's done on a resistor placed in the source circuit of the FET.

19
00:02:06,150 --> 00:02:11,550
By picking this resistor's value, we ultimately regulate the current passing through the switch.

20
00:02:13,490 --> 00:02:18,170
Not by limiting its resistance, but by means of feedback that goes to pin 3.

21
00:02:20,030 --> 00:02:26,830
And the chip, tracking the voltage drop across this resistor, changes the duration of the pulse driving the FET gate.

22
00:02:30,940 --> 00:02:34,140
Next, let's look at the parts suitable for building this converter.

23
00:02:36,320 --> 00:02:37,440
We've covered the chip.

24
00:02:38,620 --> 00:02:41,440
The PC817 optocoupler looks like this.

25
00:02:42,940 --> 00:02:48,520
By the way, for those who don't know how to count pins on chips or optocouplers, there's a dot on the case.

26
00:02:50,200 --> 00:02:51,560
That's pin 1 of the chip.

27
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:54,660
I marked the dot near pin 1.

28
00:02:57,260 --> 00:02:59,760
And the optocoupler pins correspond to the drawing.

29
00:03:01,320 --> 00:03:03,860
Full pin correspondence, same as on the chip.

30
00:03:05,780 --> 00:03:08,900
Pins are counted counterclockwise from the dot on the case.

31
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:13,320
About the switches you can use in this circuit.

32
00:03:16,060 --> 00:03:22,360
Since our supply rating will be up to 18V, the switch works in pulse mode and voltage spikes can appear.

33
00:03:27,120 --> 00:03:34,200
And so that this voltage spike doesn't break down the transistor, we need to choose it with some voltage margin, from 60V.

34
00:03:35,200 --> 00:03:37,300
In this case, I gave the name here.

35
00:03:37,620 --> 00:03:42,020
This transistor FR120N, in a package for SMD mount.

36
00:03:46,190 --> 00:03:48,750
And an IRF540, in this kind of package.

37
00:03:50,610 --> 00:03:55,050
These transistors are rated 100V, which suits us well.

38
00:03:56,050 --> 00:04:00,050
And plus they have a pretty light gate, with minimum saturation capacitance.

39
00:04:02,940 --> 00:04:04,200
Next, the diode.

40
00:04:05,440 --> 00:04:10,180
If you plan to power chips from this converter, then this diode in this package will work.

41
00:04:13,560 --> 00:04:17,340
This is a 1N5819 1A diode.

42
00:04:18,200 --> 00:04:26,040
And if you'll connect more serious loads to this converter, I recommend putting a diode in the larger 1N5822 package.

43
00:04:28,160 --> 00:04:28,960
It's 3A.

44
00:04:29,880 --> 00:04:35,520
This diode is more than enough for powering a 10-15W load, easily handles it.

45
00:04:38,950 --> 00:04:46,910
Now I need to note that the chip's power pin, here pin 7, where plus power is applied, connects directly to the transistor's that.

46
00:04:51,600 --> 00:04:53,380
Do the gate opening of the switch.

47
00:04:54,120 --> 00:04:58,380
That is, the supply voltage applied to pin 7 will also be at pin 6.

48
00:05:00,120 --> 00:05:02,360
So you can't exceed 20V here.

49
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:07,080
That's the boundary by gate breakdown.

50
00:05:08,060 --> 00:05:17,060
If by build you need this circuit to work with voltage exceeding 18V at the input, you'll need to put either an additional limiting converter or a regulator.

51
00:05:23,090 --> 00:05:33,310
On the supply to pin 7, if the rating suits, the regulator should be 18V, that is, apply no more than 18V to pin 7.

52
00:05:34,650 --> 00:05:37,310
And then you can use this chip with a higher supply rating.

53
00:05:39,230 --> 00:05:42,330
And of course, turn count will need to be picked for your supply rating.

54
00:05:44,810 --> 00:05:48,210
Now about the most important part of this converter, the core.

55
00:05:49,230 --> 00:05:56,130
The thing core in this circuit is subjected to unipolar pulses, which can easily bring it to saturation.

56
00:05:56,850 --> 00:06:00,250
So there are minimum dimensions of the core that'll work in this circuit.

57
00:06:02,850 --> 00:06:06,930
I picked this in practice, and the core dimensions are shown on this drawing.

58
00:06:09,430 --> 00:06:17,590
In length it's 15.5mm, inner radius 18.5mm, outer 29.7mm.

59
00:06:18,870 --> 00:06:21,150
The wall width is 5.6mm.

60
00:06:22,030 --> 00:06:23,230
You can also pick other cores.

61
00:06:25,530 --> 00:06:31,010
In principle, cores with bigger dimensions are welcome, with smaller ones the converter works worse.

62
00:06:31,890 --> 00:06:35,090
You can also use ferrite cup cores of corresponding dimensions.

63
00:06:36,690 --> 00:06:38,430
Now about the primary winding.

64
00:06:39,530 --> 00:06:43,790
Primary winding, turn count picked according to the supply voltage rating.

65
00:06:44,430 --> 00:06:48,170
In this case, the supplied voltage won't be higher than 18V.

66
00:06:49,170 --> 00:06:51,790
Practically it'll constantly work from 12V.

67
00:06:52,750 --> 00:06:56,630
So under this core, the optimal turn count was from 7 to 11.

68
00:06:57,990 --> 00:07:00,390
Middle value, 8 to 9 turns.

69
00:07:01,570 --> 00:07:08,030
If the turn count is too big, then the core, even at these dimensions, starts going into saturation.

70
00:07:09,490 --> 00:07:18,770
And if you reduce the turn count, for example to about 4 on this core, the current feedback triggers too early and the converter starts undervoltaging at the output.

71
00:07:21,070 --> 00:07:22,910
So you have to find a golden middle.

72
00:07:24,030 --> 00:07:27,870
Everyone will do this in practice by picking the turn count for their own core.

73
00:07:29,690 --> 00:07:31,410
That's a feature of these schemes.

74
00:07:34,130 --> 00:07:38,830
And further everything depends on the secondary winding, on what voltage we want to get.

75
00:07:40,510 --> 00:07:44,610
That is, if you need 5V, then the turn count here will be about 4.

76
00:07:45,890 --> 00:07:51,650
And if, like me in this case, you need a supply rating around 16V, that'll be 12 turns.

77
00:07:55,150 --> 00:07:56,910
So, next comes the feedback.

78
00:07:58,530 --> 00:08:05,110
I noted that the output with voltage feedback connects to the most energy-consuming section of the consumer chain.

79
00:08:06,810 --> 00:08:11,450
That is, if you assemble the circuit without this part, with you all this is fine.

80
00:08:15,790 --> 00:08:18,010
The feedback works directly on the load.

81
00:08:18,910 --> 00:08:23,830
But if you'll need to make in-secondary windings on this core, then you need to follow this rule.

82
00:08:28,160 --> 00:08:34,540
Because connecting a more powerful load to one of the leads where feedback isn't done leads to a voltage drop on it.

83
00:08:36,940 --> 00:08:41,360
Since the impact on feedback through the secondary winding happens indirectly.

84
00:08:42,540 --> 00:08:45,900
So the most powerful consumer is connected at this point.

85
00:08:47,900 --> 00:08:51,620
This way stable voltage is also achieved on other windings.

86
00:08:52,580 --> 00:08:57,580
You can also see here I introduced an 18V zener into the circuit at the output.

87
00:08:58,020 --> 00:09:07,120
The thing is, when we connect a consumer to one of this converter's windings and the PWM controller starts adapting to this load, it changes the pulse duration.

88
00:09:11,020 --> 00:09:20,320
And at pulse change in the larger direction, which usually happens when connecting a load, the voltage on the secondary windings automatically grows through inductive coupling.

89
00:09:22,480 --> 00:09:27,760
And to keep elements connected further down the circuit from breaking down, you need to add a zener here.

90
00:09:28,180 --> 00:09:32,400
In this case, a 1W zener is enough to handle this rising potential.

91
00:09:35,060 --> 00:09:41,060
At these 12 turn values, with a 10W load here, the jump can be up to 27V.

92
00:09:43,020 --> 00:09:45,820
And the 1W zener handles this potential adequately.

93
00:09:48,120 --> 00:09:49,700
Don't forget about this point.

94
00:09:51,580 --> 00:09:54,260
I also added an extra choke into the circuit.

95
00:09:54,700 --> 00:09:56,480
This isn't a mandatory system element.

96
00:09:56,900 --> 00:09:58,760
You can leave it out, but it's a filter.

97
00:10:00,520 --> 00:10:05,940
For when you need to power precision devices like chips that don't tolerate supply oscillations.

98
00:10:08,880 --> 00:10:11,340
The output rating is set by this zener.

99
00:10:14,130 --> 00:10:20,630
By changing the breakdown voltage, picking a different zener, we regulate the output voltage of the whole converter.

100
00:10:22,830 --> 00:10:31,110
If I picked a 15V zener here, then considering the optocoupler requires some voltage to trigger, the output holds about 16V.

101
00:10:34,610 --> 00:10:40,810
If you need 5V power, then put a 4.5V zener here and you'll get your 5V supply.

102
00:10:45,240 --> 00:10:48,960
At that you should reduce the secondary winding turn count of this choke.

103
00:10:50,120 --> 00:10:53,260
In this case, I used this scheme for powering a bridge inverter.

104
00:10:57,160 --> 00:11:00,680
I needed independent power that goes to the master generator.

105
00:11:02,200 --> 00:11:07,100
And four separate galvanically isolated supplies that go to control four bridge devices.

106
00:11:07,100 --> 00:11:13,330
And that's why I went with a 16V power rating.

107
00:11:15,290 --> 00:11:24,290
And if you'll build a circuit that needs different power ratings, say 5V, 9V, you change the secondary turn count and change the output zener rating.

108
00:11:28,480 --> 00:11:30,800
That'll stably hold the voltage on your load.

109
00:11:33,180 --> 00:11:35,980
I should also say a few words about mounting rules.

110
00:11:36,780 --> 00:11:43,500
Since this is a high-frequency system, the switch operating frequency here is about 160kHz.

111
00:11:44,700 --> 00:11:55,000
At these values on pin 4, this frequency setting cap of 1000pF and a resistor that creates the necessary supply voltage on the chip's clock generator.

112
00:11:56,320 --> 00:12:02,800
You should approach assembly carefully, not allowing long wires, big distances between parts on the board.

113
00:12:04,060 --> 00:12:08,740
This can lead to unwanted high-frequency pickups and inadequate converter operation.

114
00:12:09,640 --> 00:12:13,700
In this case, I assembled the circuit on this prototyping board.

115
00:12:14,280 --> 00:12:18,140
A small prototyping board like this is enough to realize the whole unit.

116
00:12:24,020 --> 00:12:25,440
Let me show its size.

117
00:12:26,740 --> 00:12:28,440
It's 6cm by 4cm.

118
00:12:30,680 --> 00:12:33,160
Practically half the board is taken by this unit.

119
00:12:34,560 --> 00:12:37,520
Its dimensions are 11 mounting holes by 14.

120
00:12:38,900 --> 00:12:43,160
You can notice how dense the assembly is, without any long wires.

121
00:12:45,640 --> 00:12:51,040
The zener that sets the output power rating and the 10Ω limiting resistor are brought out.

122
00:12:55,120 --> 00:13:00,000
Then a chain of capacitors of zeners, shown as 18V on the schematic.

123
00:13:02,380 --> 00:13:08,460
And at this point we'll have the core with wound wire, 5 leads, and a black wire of the primary winding.

124
00:13:12,600 --> 00:13:15,280
It'll be mounted in this position in this case.

125
00:13:19,780 --> 00:13:22,320
Now an interesting point about winding phasing.

126
00:13:25,380 --> 00:13:34,080
You can see the dots are in opposite directions, meaning the wires when winding the primary and secondary of the choke in one direction need to be swapped.

127
00:13:34,580 --> 00:13:42,340
If you don't do this, then at connection the converter will consume strongly, and incorrect winding connection will show up as heating of this diode.

128
00:13:44,340 --> 00:13:47,060
This will be palpable if you touch it with your finger.

129
00:13:47,420 --> 00:13:49,400
It can heat up to about 50 degrees.

130
00:13:50,080 --> 00:13:55,120
On polarity change you'll immediately see an adequate rise in output voltage.

131
00:13:55,580 --> 00:14:02,360
This diode will be cold, and the converter will switch to no-load economical consumption mode at 0.02A.

132
